{
  "dom_id": "step-q94809",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $J$ be the set of purely imaginary complex numbers, that is, complex numbers of the form  $ai,$ where  $a$ is a nonzero real number. Complete the following sentence: \n\n*$J$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of complex numbers and scalar multiplication of a complex number by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*",
  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$J$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to addition of complex numbers and scalar multiplication of a complex number by a real number) because **multiplication of $z\\in J$ by real scalars is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =0$ and $z=i,$ then $\\alpha \\cdot z=0\\cdot i=0\\notin J.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition in $J$ is associative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition  in $J$ is commutative.  $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication in $J$ satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- If  $z\\in J$ then $z=ai$ for some $a\\in R.$ Hence, $-z=(-a)i\\in J$   since $-a\\in R.$ $\\,\u2713$",
